			"color":"#322",
			"widgets_values":[
				"Create a 15-second cute, funny, educational 3D animated video for children ages 3 to 7.\n\nConcept: Teach the letter C using the word “Cat” through two major visual highlights:\n\n1. A slow, satisfying transformation from the word “CAT” into a cute kitten.\n2. A slow, magical transformation from a colorful stylized 3D animated world into a realistic natural scene with a real-looking cat.\n\nMain learning message:\n\n“C is for Cat.”\n\nVisual style:\n\nHigh-end cinematic 3D family animation with premium feature-film quality, soft rounded shapes, expressive character design, large friendly eyes, colorful environments, warm cinematic lighting, smooth polished animation, gentle depth of field, soft shadows, detailed materials, playful humor, and emotional storytelling.\n\nUse a charming storybook-inspired 3D animation style with pastel colors, clean composition, rounded forms, expressive facial animation, soft atmospheric lighting, and a warm child-friendly tone.\n\nTiming and scene flow:\n\n0:00–0:02\n\nOpen on a dreamy colorful 3D animated garden with a pastel blue sky, fluffy clouds, flowers, green grass, small bushes, floating sparkles, and warm sunlight.\n\nA large uppercase letter “C” appears in the center of the screen. A smaller lowercase “c” appears beside it. Both letters bounce gently into place with cheerful energy.\n\nThe narrator says slowly and clearly:\n\n“C is for Cat.”\n\nHold the letters long enough for children to recognize them.\n\n0:02–0:04\n\nThe word “CAT” writes itself slowly across the screen in large, rounded, colorful 3D letters.\n\nThe “C” has a soft orange gradient, the “A” has colorful stripes and tiny paw-print patterns, and the “T” has a gentle pastel texture.\n\nKeep the word “CAT” centered, stable, correctly spelled, and fully readable.\n\nLet the audience admire the complete word before the transformation begins.\n\n0:04–0:09\n\nThis is the main visual highlight.\n\nThe word “CAT” slowly and satisfyingly transforms into a cute kitten.\n\nThe transformation must happen gradually, one letter at a time:\n\nThe “C” gently opens and curves into the kitten’s fluffy tail.\n\nThe “A” stretches upward and reshapes into the kitten’s head, ears, and pointed cheeks.\n\nThe “T” bends and transforms into the kitten’s body, four small legs, and soft paws.\n\nThe colorful letter textures flow naturally into the kitten’s fur.\n\nDo not transform all the letters at once. Let each letter morph in a smooth chain reaction.\n\nThe child should still recognize the original word “CAT” while it is changing.\n\nThe letters should bend, stretch, curl, rotate, soften, unfold, and glide into their new forms.\n\nUse sparkling trails, flowing color transfer, soft glowing particles, gentle squash-and-stretch animation, and satisfying elastic motion.\n\nThe transformation should feel magical, clear, playful, and easy for young children to understand.\n\n0:09–0:11\n\nThe transformation is complete.\n\nA fully formed cute stylized 3D kitten appears in the garden.\n\nThe kitten has soft orange-and-white fur, big expressive eyes, tiny rounded paws, perky ears, a fluffy tail, a small pink nose, and a friendly smiling expression.\n\nThe kitten takes one curious step, tries to catch a floating butterfly, misses, becomes slightly surprised, then calmly pretends that it meant to do that.\n\nKeep the humor gentle and safe for young children.\n\nDisplay the word “Cat” below the kitten briefly for reinforcement.\n\nThe narrator repeats warmly:\n\n“Cat.”\n\n0:11–0:14\n\nThis is the second major visual highlight.\n\nThe entire colorful 3D animated garden slowly transforms into a realistic natural scene.\n\nThe kitten blinks and gently moves its tail. With each movement, realism gradually increases:\n\nThe animated fur becomes detailed, soft, and naturally textured.\n\nThe eyes gain realistic reflections while remaining friendly.\n\nThe paws and body gain believable weight and natural movement.\n\nThe flowers become real flowers with detailed petals.\n\nThe grass develops realistic blades, shadows, and subtle movement.\n\nThe pastel background becomes a real sunlit garden with natural depth of field.\n\nThe lighting becomes warm and natural.\n\nA gentle breeze moves the grass and the kitten’s fur.\n\nThe transformation must feel seamless, breathtaking, immersive, and emotionally warm.\n\nDo not cut abruptly from the animated world to the realistic world. Let the scene bloom into reality gradually in layers.\n\nThe viewer should feel that imagination is turning into nature.\n\n0:14–0:15\n\nEnd on a beautiful realistic cat sitting peacefully beside a real flower in warm sunlight.\n\nThe cat looks toward the camera, blinks once, gives a gentle head tilt, and softly flicks its tail.\n\nDisplay the text clearly:\n\n“C is for Cat”\n\nThe narrator says warmly and clearly:\n\n“C is for Cat.”\n\nThe cat gives one soft, friendly meow.\n\nStyle and quality requirements:\n\nMaintain a premium cinematic 3D family animation look during the opening and middle sections.\n\nUse expressive facial animation, polished character movement, soft cinematic lighting, colorful materials, smooth camera motion, beautiful depth of field, and clean visual storytelling.\n\nKeep the letter “C” and the word “CAT” clearly readable before and during the transformation.\n\nMake the word-to-cat morph slow, rich, clear, and visually rewarding.\n\nMake the stylized-to-realistic transformation equally slow, smooth, seamless, and magical.\n\nMaintain consistent kitten design, fur color, facial features, and body proportions.\n\nCamera direction:\n\nBegin with a centered front-facing composition.\n\nDuring the word transformation, slowly push in toward the letters and kitten.\n\nUse a gentle close-up when the kitten reacts humorously.\n\nDuring the realism transition, slowly dolly forward while keeping the kitten as the main subject.\n\nAvoid excessive camera shake, rapid zooms, chaotic movement, or confusing framing.\n\nAudio direction:\n\nUse cheerful, soft, magical orchestral music with light marimba, pizzicato strings, gentle bells, and warm woodwind instruments.\n\nAdd delicate sparkle sounds when the letters appear.\n\nAdd soft pops, magical whooshes, stretching sounds, and gentle unfolding sounds during the transformation.\n\nAdd tiny playful paw steps, a soft comedic reaction sound, and one friendly kitten meow.\n\nDuring the realism transition, gradually introduce natural garden ambience, soft wind, birds, rustling grass, and subtle outdoor sounds.\n\nThe narrator’s voice should be warm, clear, friendly, slow, and easy for children ages 3 to 7 to follow.\n\nNarration:\n\n0:00–0:02:\n“C is for Cat.”\n\n0:09–0:11:\n“Cat.”\n\n0:14–0:15:\n“C is for Cat.”\n\nAvoid:\n\nRushed transformations, abrupt cuts, unreadable letters, incorrect spelling, distorted typography, chaotic effects, cluttered backgrounds, scary expressions, aggressive animals, horror, dark frightening scenes, excessive camera movement, inconsistent kitten design, broken anatomy, extra characters, random objects, random words, misspelled text, logos, watermarks, subtitles other than the requested text, flickering, visual noise, or anything confusing for young children."
			],
